it's so new they're still moving into  this manufacturing and development  facility in coventry  the only center of its kind in the uk  they make batteries for everything from  trains to planes  and electric cars in the city that gave  birth to the motor industry  there's a sense of pride at being part  of the journey to net zero  this facility forms part of the jigsaw  of activities required  to develop technology and and drive that  through  the market and which which will reduce  climate change in the uk and the rest of  the world in fact in time this hall will  be  full of people manufacturing and packing  batteries the prime minister wants  facilities like this to help make  britain  a world leader on tackling climate  change  overall this is a package which is part  of  turbocharging the green industrial  revolution that the prime minister set  out  levelling up across our country the  government says it wants places like the  west midlands with its manufacturing  heritage  to be at the forefront of the green  revolution but compared to other  countries there are questions about how  much  money boris johnson is spending on his  10-point  climate plan the government's own  advisory body the committee on climate  change  says between 20 and 40 billion pounds a  year  needs to be spent to get to net zero by  2050  the think tank the ippr puts the figure  at 33 billion  but the new climate plan commits to 12  billion and only a third of that is new  money  i don't think what's been announced uh  is enough to get us to net zero  we set out what we think he's needed to  get there which is 33  billion pounds a year just a few weeks  ago the government had only invested  a tenth of that so far and what's been  announced today another 4 billion  will help but it will not bridge the gap  critics say the spending scatters money  on new technology such as nuclear energy  and  carbon capture without enough focus on  major issues like decarbonizing our  homes  public transport and nature based  solutions  there's a lot of green talk but not  enough green  action if you like the rhetoric and the  reality  are quite far divorced and what we need  is is with is more urgency  more ambition and a credible and  comprehensive plan  britain is changing as we try to reduce  the amount of emissions we produce  the 10-point plan gives a new sense of  momentum  but we're entering uncharted territory  about how best to tackle the climate  emergency  lisa holland sky news
 

